<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_create_constraint.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-createconstraint">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-createconstraint">
  <primary>CREATE CONSTRAINT</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE CONSTRAINT</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE CONSTRAINT</refname>
  <refpurpose>require a value of a label to be unique, or to satisfy a condition</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE CONSTRAINT [ <replaceable class="parameter">constraint_name</replaceable> ] ON <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> ASSERT <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> IS UNIQUE
CREATE CONSTRAINT [ <replaceable class="parameter">constraint_name</replaceable> ] ON <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> ASSERT <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createconstraint-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   There are two forms, and they become two different things on disk.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>ASSERT <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> IS
   UNIQUE</literal> becomes an exclusion constraint over the property read, such as
   <literal>EXCLUDE USING btree ((properties.'title') WITH =)</literal>. A write whose value
   already exists is rejected as a conflict.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>ASSERT <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable></literal> becomes a
   check constraint over the same kind of read, such as
   <literal>CHECK ((properties.'age')::integer &gt; 9)</literal>. A write whose row fails the
   condition is rejected.
  </para>

  <para>
   The name is optional in both forms. Left out, one is derived:
   <literal><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable>_unique_constraint</literal>
   for the unique form and
   <literal><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable>_properties_check</literal>
   for the check form, with a numeric suffix added if that name is taken. A name that is
   supplied has to be free in the graph's schema, because the unique form creates an index of
   that name.
  </para>

  <para>
   The label is looked up in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>, and its name
   cannot be qualified. Both forms work on a vertex label and on an edge label. Neither works
   on <literal>ag_vertex</literal> or <literal>ag_edge</literal>, which refuse the change with
   <literal>ALTER base vertex label is prohibited</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   A constraint is created over the rows already in the label, so an existing row that
   violates it makes the statement fail. A check constraint is a SQL check
   constraint, so a missing property satisfies it. The
   condition evaluates to unknown for an absent property, and a check constraint accepts
   unknown. Use the constraint to restrict values, not to require a key.
  </para>

  <para>
   There is no replace. <literal>CREATE OR REPLACE CONSTRAINT</literal> is parsed and then
   refused with <literal>CREATE OR REPLACE CONSTRAINT is not supported</literal>. To change a
   constraint, drop it and create it again.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createconstraint-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">constraint_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name for the constraint. Optional. For the unique form it also names the index, so it
      must not collide with an existing relation in the graph.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Label the constraint applies to, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The value that has to be unique. Usually one property, written the way Cypher writes
      it: <literal>title</literal>, or <literal>name.first</literal> for a key inside a
      nested map. Any Cypher expression over the label's properties is accepted, so
      <literal>ASSERT toLower(trim(id)) IS UNIQUE</literal> makes the folded value unique
      rather than the stored one. See <xref linkend="cypher-expr"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A Cypher condition over the label's properties. It is evaluated once for each written
      element. A subquery is refused with
      <literal>cannot use subquery in check constraint</literal>, and so is a parameter.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createconstraint-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They
   build a graph of their own and drop it at the end:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH constrain;
SET graph_path = constrain;
CREATE VLABEL film;
CREATE ELABEL rated;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Require a property to be unique:
<programlisting>
CREATE CONSTRAINT film_title_uniq ON film ASSERT title IS UNIQUE;

CREATE CONSTRAINT
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The first write goes in. The second one carries the same title, and the message names the
   constraint and the value:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:film {title: 'Arrival', year: 2016});
CREATE (:film {title: 'Arrival', year: 2021});

ERROR:  conflicting key value violates exclusion constraint "film_title_uniq"
DETAIL:  Key ((properties.'title'))=("Arrival") conflicts with existing key ((properties.'title'))=("Arrival").
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Leave the name out and one is derived. This second constraint on
   <literal>film</literal> is called <literal>film_unique_constraint</literal>:
<programlisting>
CREATE CONSTRAINT ON film ASSERT year IS UNIQUE;

CREATE CONSTRAINT
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Restrict what a property may hold. The cast makes the comparison an integer one:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL teen;
CREATE CONSTRAINT teen_age ON teen ASSERT (10 &lt;= age::int AND age::int &lt;= 19);

CREATE CONSTRAINT
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A row inside the range is written. One outside it is not:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:teen {name: 'Kim', age: 15});
CREATE (:teen {name: 'Lee', age: 20});

ERROR:  new row for relation "teen" violates check constraint "teen_age"
DETAIL:  Failing row contains (5.2, {"age": 20, "name": "Lee"}).
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A vertex with no <literal>age</literal> at all is accepted, because the condition is
   unknown rather than false:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:teen {name: 'Bo'});
MATCH (t:teen) RETURN t.name AS name, t.age AS age ORDER BY name;

 name  | age 
-------+-----
 "Bo"  | 
 "Kim" | 15
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge label takes either form on its own properties:
<programlisting>
CREATE CONSTRAINT rated_pair ON rated ASSERT source IS UNIQUE;

CREATE CONSTRAINT
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The constraint is checked against the rows that are already there, so it cannot be created
   over rows that break it. The unique form reports the pair it found, and the check form
   reports only that some row failed:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL rerun;
CREATE (:rerun {title: 'Arrival'}), (:rerun {title: 'Arrival'});
CREATE CONSTRAINT rerun_t ON rerun ASSERT title IS UNIQUE;

ERROR:  could not create exclusion constraint "rerun_t"
DETAIL:  Key ((properties.'title'))=("Arrival") conflicts with key ((properties.'title'))=("Arrival").
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Two refusals worth recognizing. There is no replace, and a unique constraint's name is an
   index name, so reusing one raises
   <literal>relation "film_title_uniq" already exists</literal>:
<programlisting>
CREATE OR REPLACE CONSTRAINT film_title_uniq ON film ASSERT title IS UNIQUE;

ERROR:  CREATE OR REPLACE CONSTRAINT is not supported
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   And the label has to exist. This statement does not create one the way
   <literal>CREATE (:film)</literal> does:
<programlisting>
CREATE CONSTRAINT c ON nosuch ASSERT a IS UNIQUE;

ERROR:  label "nosuch" does not exist
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH constrain CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createconstraint-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   A unique constraint is an exclusion constraint, not a unique index, so it is not
   interchangeable with <literal>CREATE UNIQUE PROPERTY INDEX</literal>. Both reject a
   duplicate. The error differs:
   <literal>conflicting key value violates exclusion constraint</literal> against
   <literal>duplicate key value violates unique constraint</literal>. So does the statement
   that removes it. A constraint is dropped by <xref linkend="cypher-dropconstraint"/>, a
   unique index by <xref linkend="cypher-droppropertyindex"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The two forms differ over label inheritance. A check constraint is inherited, so a child
   label created after it carries the constraint and is checked by it. A unique constraint is
   not. A child label gets no exclusion constraint, so the same value can be written once
   through the parent and once through the child, and a <command>MATCH</command> on the
   parent then returns both. To make a value unique across a hierarchy, create the constraint
   on each label in it. Nothing makes one value unique across several labels, because a
   constraint, like the index behind it, covers one table.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createconstraint-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no constraint DDL. It constrains a graph with a graph type, created
   by the statement in clause 12.6, which declares the labels and properties a graph's
   elements may have. A graph type has no rule for the uniqueness of one property and no
   check condition, and a graph is constrained at all only if it references a graph type.
   AgensGraph graphs are open, so <command>CREATE CONSTRAINT</command> is an AgensGraph
   extension.
  </para>

  <para>
   The check form corresponds to
   <literal>ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT ... CHECK</literal>, and the unique form to
   <literal>ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT ... EXCLUDE USING btree (... WITH =)</literal>. It
   does not correspond to <literal>UNIQUE</literal>, which cannot be written over an
   expression. The condition is a Cypher expression rather than a SQL one, so
   <literal>age</literal> means the property, not a column.
   <literal>NOT NULL</literal>, <literal>PRIMARY KEY</literal>,
   <literal>FOREIGN KEY</literal>, <literal>DEFERRABLE</literal> and
   <literal>NOT VALID</literal>, which <literal>ADD CONSTRAINT</literal> offers, have no
   spelling here.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

